1
00:00:10,199 --> 00:00:15,559
This is HBCField, open at the desk of
a company that repairs things on other

2
00:00:15,559 --> 00:00:21,413
people's premises. Everything you are
about to see belongs to one organisation: one

3
00:00:21,413 --> 00:00:27,196
company, one account, and a wall around
the data that nothing crosses by accident.

4
00:00:27,648 --> 00:00:34,874
Four words carry the whole product. An
organisation. Its workspaces. The people in

5
00:00:34,874 --> 00:00:42,277
it. And the work. Learn those four, and
every screen after this one explains itself.

6
00:00:42,745 --> 00:00:48,667
The organisation is the company itself.
It is what you signed up as, what you are

7
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:54,515
billed as, and the boundary your data
sits inside. Nobody outside it sees any of

8
00:00:54,515 --> 00:00:57,513
this unless you deliberately open a door.

9
00:00:57,966 --> 00:01:04,989
Inside the organisation are workspaces. A
workspace is somewhere the work happens,

10
00:01:04,989 --> 00:01:11,841
or a part of the business that runs its
own way. A depot. A workshop. A client's

11
00:01:11,841 --> 00:01:12,269
site.

12
00:01:12,726 --> 00:01:18,883
A workspace is not a folder. Each one
switches on only the parts of the product it

13
00:01:18,883 --> 00:01:25,041
actually needs, and is billed for only
those. This one keeps track of vehicles and

14
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:30,748
of hours worked. A workspace that
needs neither turns both off, and pays for

15
00:01:30,748 --> 00:01:31,348
neither.

16
00:01:31,802 --> 00:01:38,175
And one of these is not yours at all. It
is a client's site. In this trade people

17
00:01:38,175 --> 00:01:44,548
spend the whole day on somebody else's
premises, so that site is a workplace like

18
00:01:44,548 --> 00:01:48,010
any other, and the
product treats it as one.

19
00:01:48,463 --> 00:01:55,078
Then the people. Some of them work at a
desk. Some are out in a van from seven in

20
00:01:55,078 --> 00:02:01,939
the morning. And some do not work for you
at all — a client's supervisor, allowed in

21
00:02:01,939 --> 00:02:05,042
to follow the job they are paying for.

22
00:02:05,496 --> 00:02:11,591
What somebody can reach is not decided by
their job title. It is a role you build

23
00:02:11,591 --> 00:02:17,912
yourself: a list of what they may do, and
which workspaces they may do it in. Change

24
00:02:17,912 --> 00:02:21,750
the role, and everybody
holding it changes with it.

25
00:02:22,204 --> 00:02:28,281
And here is the work. A job says what
needs doing, where, by when, and who has it.

26
00:02:28,281 --> 00:02:34,209
Every other screen in this product is
either getting ready for a job, or reading

27
00:02:34,209 --> 00:02:36,136
back what happened on one.

28
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:42,899
Open one, and it is the whole story on a
page. Who is on it. Everything anybody did

29
00:02:42,899 --> 00:02:49,284
to it, in order. The conversation that
went with it. And at the end, the report: the

30
00:02:49,284 --> 00:02:53,541
hours, the parts fitted, and
the client's own signature.

31
00:02:53,995 --> 00:03:00,640
There are two ways in. The office works
in a browser, like this one. The people in

32
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:07,286
the field work on a phone, and see only
their own day on it. One organisation, two

33
00:03:07,286 --> 00:03:10,528
windows, and the same facts behind both.

34
00:03:10,981 --> 00:03:17,529
That is the shape of it. Next: the
workspaces everything else hangs off.

